WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
Hey everyone, welcome to this explainer
covering the first module of

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.360
National University's ANA 605 course.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:11.000
We know how tough it is juggling your
education with your career, your family,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.600
and your life obligations.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.120
We honor the hard work you are doing.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.320
Our goal is to support you by taking
advanced statistical concepts

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.800
and transforming them into direct,
practical career upgrades.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.880
We are moving from foundational theory
into applied professional data science.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.600
Let's get into it.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Here is our roadmap for today.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.320
We are looking at four main stops.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.600
First, the data science mission.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.800
Second, mastering reproducible
reporting.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.800
Third, building multivariable models.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.000
And finally, translating data to action.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.500
We will see how R Markdown and
multivariable regression serve

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.000
a singular professional purpose.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.000
Starting with Section 1,
the data science mission.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.100
Specifically, what is the true goal
of your profession?

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.000
Before we hit the heavy math and coding,
we need to anchor ourselves

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.500
to this core mission.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.100
Professor Matthew Vanderbilt hits the
nail on the head here.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.000
He says, fundamentally, data science
is all about minimizing uncertainty.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.500
That is all we do.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.000
Whether you are writing a simple model
or training a massive AI,

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.500
your existence is dedicated to this task.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.540
What do we mean by uncertainty?

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.000
We are talking about statistical
variance and the lack of actionable

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.000
information that stops leaders from
making confident decisions.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.000
The takeaway is that we are not just
doing math to find a P-value.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
We are trying to reduce uncertainty
so stakeholders can take action.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Mastering reproducible reporting
or upgrading your workflow

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.000
with R Markdown.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
To reduce uncertainty for others,
you must eliminate it from your

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.500
own workflow first.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.000
This is a game changer for your job.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.000
Think about the old workflow.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.000
You get data, analyze it, and manually
copy and paste charts into Word.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.000
That process is exhausting

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.000
and prone to human error.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.000
But when you master R Markdown files,
you can turn that grueling process

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
into a 30-second automated render.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.500
You get the data, hit render, and boom.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.000
Perfect, error-free output.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.000
How does it work?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.000
The architecture is fascinating.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
It uses YAML headers for metadata.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.000
Then you have your narrative text
mixed with executable code chunks.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.000
Those can run R, Python, or SQL.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
The magic is dynamic inline code.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Instead of manually typing a value,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.000
your text pulls the live value
from the data.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.000
If your data updates, your narrative
updates automatically.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Zero manual data entry.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.000
To make reports executive-ready,
master chunk execution options.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.000
By default, code and output are visible.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.000
In the real world, use echo equals false
to hide the messy code.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.000
Or use include equals false to hide
background tasks.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.000
This gives the C-suite clean,
actionable insights.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.000
On to Section 3, building
multivariable models.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.000
This is about isolating impact to
reduce business variance.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.000
We are strictly focusing on
multivariable models.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.000
That means multiple inputs predicting
one single outcome.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.000
That is different from multivariate
regression.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.000
We want multivariable models to
isolate the effect of one predictor.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.000
Why not dump every piece of data
into our model?

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.000
It is tempting to see what sticks.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.000
But we must prioritize the simplest
effective model.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.000
Two dangers: overfitting and
multicollinearity.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.000
Overfitting memorizes the training data,
making it useless for the future.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.000
Multicollinearity happens when inputs
have linear relationships.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.000
This artificially inflates success.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.000
You might see a 98% R-squared value
and think it is great.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.000
But your model is broken.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.000
Use variance inflation factors, or VIF,
to detect this overlap.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.000
Rip the redundant variable out.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.000
Keep your model simple.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.000
Section 4, translating data to action.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.000
Doing math in a vacuum helps no one.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.000
Apply this to your work challenges.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.000
But be protective of your career.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.000
Never violate confidentiality.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.000
Use synthetic or public data to
preserve security.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.000
Level up your career safely.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.000
Let's talk about the AI policy.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.000
We encourage using tools like Claude
for learning and coding help.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.000
Prompt, synthesize, and disclose.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.000
Drop your prompts at the bottom of
your assignment.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.000
AI is prohibited during exams.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.000
Finally, executive communication.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.000
Translate technical outputs into
plain language.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.000
Follow APA7 formatting.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.000
Focus on actionable information.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.000
Keep this question in mind.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.000
If the CEO cannot understand your
model, did you reduce uncertainty?

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.000
Mastering that translation is the
real secret.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.000
That makes you invaluable.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:20.000
Thanks for joining this explainer.
